%section.hero.container
  %h1.hero-title
    Ruby 4.0, written in Rust.
  %p.hero-lede
    Metorex runs Ruby 4.0 programs, and it exposes a method's syntax tree as objects your program can read at runtime.
  .hero-sample
    .sample-label example.rb
    :plain
      <pre><code class="language-metorex">Point = Struct.new(:x, :y) do
        def +(other) = Point.new(x + other.x, y + other.y)
      end

      total = [Point.new(1, 2), Point.new(3, 4)].sum(Point.new(0, 0))
      puts total.inspect

      case total
      in { x: Integer =&gt; x, y: } if y &gt; x
        puts "y leads by #{y - x}"
      end</code></pre>
    .sample-label Output
    :plain
      <pre class="sample-output"><code>#&lt;struct Point x=4, y=6&gt;
      y leads by 2</code></pre>

%section.features.container
  .feature-card
    %h2 Ruby 4.0 syntax
    :plain
      <p>Metorex reads Ruby 4.0 source with its own lexer and parser. <code>RUBY_VERSION</code> reports <code>4.0.6</code>.</p>
  .feature-card
    %h2 Code as objects
    %p A method's body is available at runtime as syntax tree nodes you can inspect.
  .feature-card
    %h2 Built-in test runner
    :plain
      <p><code>metorex --test DIR</code> finds and runs <code>*_test.rb</code>, <code>test_*.rb</code>, and <code>*_spec.rb</code> files.</p>
  .feature-card
    %h2 C extensions
    %p Loads C extensions written against Ruby's C API.
  .feature-card
    %h2 REPL
    :plain
      <p><code>metorex --repl</code> starts an interactive session.</p>
  .feature-card
    %h2 Written in Rust
    %p The lexer, parser, and bytecode VM are written in Rust.

%section.notice.container
  %p
    %strong Metorex is in early development.
    Programs that run under MRI may not run under Metorex yet.
    %a{href: 'https://github.com/metorex-lang/metorex/issues/new'} Open an issue
    for one that fails, or
    %a{href: 'https://github.com/metorex-lang/metorex/pulls'} send a pull request.
